  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nEarlier this evening an upper ridge was located over the \nCentral Plains while our next, significant, upper level \ntrough/low was located near the coast of California. Hurricane \nPolo was located just west of Baja California. Hurricane Polo \nremains forecast to weaken after it makes landfall on the west \ncoast of the Baja California Peninsula late Monday and by \nTuesday night is forecast to be moving across southern New \nMexico and extreme western Kansas. As remnants of Hurricane Polo\nmoves northeast toward New Mexico/West Texas towards mid week \nthe California upper level trough will move across the southwest\nUnited States. Ahead of this developing storm system to our \nsouthwest there will be an significant increase in moisture to \nsouthwest Kansas due to southerly moisture return from the Gulf\ncombined with tropical moisture that will be accompanying the \nremnants of Polo. PWAT values are forecast to be near 100% of \nclimatology across southwest Kansas by Tuesday. This abundant \nmoisture when combined with the approaching upper level storm \nsystem from the southwest will favor an extended period of \nwidespread measurable rainfall to southwest Kansas from early \nTuesday through Thursday. Heavy rainfall potential for portions \nof extreme southwest Kansas will also be possible and is high \nenough that the weather prediction central has issued an \nmoderate risk of excessive rainfall. This means that there is at\nleast a 40% risk of rainfall exceeding flash flood guidance \nwithin 25 miles of a point.\n\nRainfall Totals. There is still some uncertainty on rainfall \ntotals from this upcoming even given that models continue to \nindicated subtle differences in timing and track of the upper \ntrough/low and how it interacts with the remnants of Polo. Still\ntaking these uncertainties into account it appears all of \nsouthwest Kansas will likely (>60%) receive greater than 1.5 \ninches from this upcoming event before the rain tapers off on \nThursday night. This morning all the ensembles to various \ndegrees are now focusing on the area with the highest rainfall \npotential to be located west and north of the Dodge City given \nthe longer period of rainfall potential and location/track of \nthe upper level deformation zone and moisture associated with \nthe upper low/trough as it moves from New Mexico on Wednesday \ninto Central Plains and weakens by Thursday night. As a result \nthis is why there is a greater than 60% chance at this time for \nrainfall totals from this event to be greater than 2.5 inches \nalong and west of highway 83 and north of the I-70 corridor. It \nis also why a flood watch for flash flooding has been issued \nfor portions of extreme southwest Kansas from Tuesday morning\nthrough Wednesday morning. Elsewhere across southwest and south\ncentral Kansas the rainfall totals are expect to be less but do\nnot be fooled because ensembles still indicated at 30% to 60% \nchance for rainfall totals to exceed 1.5 inches in these \nlocations as well. At this time anyone with plans mid week \nshould be prepared for an extended period of soaking rainfall \nwith period of heavy rainfall being possible. Water issues could\ndevelop, especially west of highway 83 and near the I-70 \ncorridor.\n\nSouthwest Kansas prior to the onset of this rainfall late \nMonday night through Thursday night can look forward to one more\ndry day with seasonal temperature today. Early morning status \nand fog is expected to dissipate between 10am and noon. The fog 18\nearly this morning may become dense in a areas west and north of\nDodge City given the moist upslope flow that will be present. \nAfter good collaboration with our neighbors we have decided to \nhold off on a dense fog advisory at this time but do not be \nsurprised if a dense fog advisory is issued later this morning.",
